What's behind #Ethiopia obsession with Naval Capability? In their own words, to use as instrument to change boundaries! Believe them when they tell you!

Per HornReview Article, naval capability would play a significant role in projecting power with the ability to aﬀect “the strategic balance within the Horn of Africa...naval capabilities serve as powerful bargaining chips in international negotiations over port access, maritime boundaries, and security partnerships.” 

the above is a quote from an article authored by #HornReview on November 13, 2025. As everyone by now knows, HornReview is affiliated with Ethiopia's ministry of foreign affairs and claims to have partnership with the War College of the Ethiopian National Defense Force. Blen Mamo, the Executive Director of HornReview, also works for the MFA’s Institute of Foreign Aﬀairs.

Honing in into that quote makes it clear that they intend to use whatever naval power they develop as instrument of negotiations for sovereign port ownership and maritime boundaries! This expansionist ambition is what is at play, not commercial access! It's quite so clear & needs no further elaboration! How audacious of them to brag about their intentions out in the open!

Given the backlash, now their "enhanced" childish plan goes something like this: we start with a commercial access & lease a naval base (not sovereign) to allay suspicions from our coastal partners. Then once our navy is strong enough, within 2-3 years, we can initiate leveraging that naval power to demand the coastal state hand over sovereignty of the port to us. Where does this 2-3 years come from? I didn't make it up! an IFA article from October 16, 2026 specifically discusses implementation road map, including naval capabilities in 3-10 years (see screenshot).

Abiy is only interested in naval power! Commercial access is irrelevant to him! In fact, Ethiopia has never been denied commercial access by any of its coastal state neighbors!

#Abiy was offered to jointly manage a commercial port with #Djibouti in 2024. Per president Guelleh, Abiy rejected the plan and counter-proposed that the deal include a naval base plus a narrow, extra-territorial strip/corridor all the way to the Ethiopian border. This strip was to be managed/administrated by Ethiopia as if it's part of its sovereignty. Guelleh obviously rejected that. #Somalia's president HSM also offered to negotiate a commercial port. So far, Abiy has not shown any enthusiasm to that. #Eritrea too never has refused commercial access on the basis of international law and UNCLOS. 

So, the media may act as if the tension started last month and run with the narrative spread by the actual aggressor. Perhaps b/c media naturally tends to fan conflicts, to sensationalize matters and is devoid of nuanced articles. After all, nuanced articles are not click-baiters. But the media erred when it ran with Ethiopia's unverified, blatant lies instead of taking a minute to check and hear the other side of the story. So, who can blame Eritreans for believing the media was deliberately spreading falsehoods & perhaps it's in cahoots with those backing Abiy's dangerous war projects!

*Note: the screenshots are from Institute of Foreign Affairs' article published on October 16, 2026. So, Abiy's regime may speak of "peaceful diplomacy" but the intent and focus is clearly on naval power to shape the region and change boundaries, with Eritrea being the primary target!

